From aea8d9cf50f86bacd4259f0e1c7855b4b3e21eaa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 20:57:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: buffers of more than 8 bits that Kodi
 allocates itself, and the window's queue with them

nvidia-drm with the 580 driver (GTX 10 and older) takes buffers of 10 bits (AB30, XB30) and of half
floats (AB4H) on its planes, and its EGL offers windows of 8 bits only (a user's log: "no 10-bit
output; output formats ... AB30:plane XB30:plane AB4H:plane; EGL window configs: R5G6B5A0:RG16
R8G8B8A0:XR24 R8G8B8A8:AR24"): HDR10 went out at 8 bits, with "max bpc" 12.

COwnScanout makes three such buffers with GBM (scanout and rendering, with the modifiers that the
plane takes for the format), imports each into EGL (EGL_EXT_image_dma_buf_import, with its modifier
where EGL_EXT_image_dma_buf_import_modifiers is there), binds it to a renderbuffer
(glEGLImageTargetRenderbufferStorageOES) and a framebuffer object, and checks it before anything is
shown through it: the framebuffer is complete, and four pixels cleared to values that 8 bits cannot
hold (10-bit codes such as 1, 513 and 1021; half floats in steps of 2^-10 to 2^-12) come back as
they were written. Any step that fails is said in plain words, and nothing is left of what was made.

They stand for the window's buffers with the semantics of a gbm_surface (IOwnBuffers): the frame is
drawn into the back buffer, "swapped", the front one is locked for Kodi's commit and given back when
Kodi no longer holds it (COwnBufferRing). CGBMSurface takes them in place of the window's
(SetOwnBuffers()), so that every place that locks the front buffer for a commit takes them as it
takes the window's. Three buffers, as Kodi holds of the window on nvidia-drm: the one on the screen,
the one whose flip comes next, and the one drawn into.

The queue of CGBMSurface gave back one buffer when the window said it had none free. With buffers of
both kinds in it (just after a switch, either way), giving back one of the other kind frees none of
the kind that is drawn into: the oldest are given back until one is free, never the newest; with one
kind alone it is one, as before.

Tested on the host (Mesa, ownscanout_test.cpp, a test program of this change: GBMUtils.cpp as it is, a
gbm_surface of three buffers as egl-gbm, OwnScanout.cpp's GL steps): with llvmpipe, 20 of 20: a
buffer to draw into in every frame across both switches, never one on the screen or whose flip comes
next, every buffer given back; 10-bit and half-float images keep the values, 8-bit ones fail the probe,
as they must. Without the loop, the switch to Kodi's own buffers runs out of a buffer at its third
frame; with the loop as first written (it ended with the last buffer of the other kind), the switch
back does: both fail the test. zink on lavapipe draws wrongly into EGL images (its own warning: no
EXT_image_drm_format_modifier) and the probe says so (it fails); it imports no dma-buf. Not tested
anywhere: the GBM allocation for scanout and the import of a GBM buffer (no GPU in the build host).
